{
  "title": [
    "deltaclip~"
  ],
  "description": "limit changes between samples",
  "origin": "cyclone",
  "categories": [
    "Signal Math",
    "Effects"
  ],
  "inlets": [
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "signal",
          "description": "any signal whose changes will be clipped"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    },
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "float/signal",
          "description": "maximum delta in decreasing signal"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    },
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "float/signal",
          "description": "maximum delta in increasing signal"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    }
  ],
  "outlets": [
    {
      "messages": [
        {
          "type": "signal",
          "description": "input with its change limited to the minimum and maximum delta"
        }
      ],
      "repeat": false
    }
  ],
  "arguments": [
    {
      "type": "float",
      "description": "delta minimum - typically negative",
      "default": "0"
    },
    {
      "type": "float",
      "description": "delta maximum - typically positive",
      "default": "0"
    }
  ],
  "flags": [],
  "methods": [
    {
      "type": "reset",
      "description": "sets the last value to \"0"
    }
  ],
  "body": "[deltaclip~] limits the change between samples in an incoming signal. This is also known as 'slew limiting'. It has a negative maximum delta for when the signal decays and a positive maximum delta for when it rises. When they're both 0, the signal doesn't shift. Divide by the sample rate to set the maximum change per second instead of per sample."
}